<p><strong>Vouchers and packages stop causing arguments when each has a unique code or record, a live balance and an expiry that every branch can see.</strong></p>
<p><em>The scene below is a typical one, not a single named customer.</em></p>
<h2>"I still have 2,000 on my voucher"</h2>
<p>A customer arrives with a gift voucher bought months ago. She believes there is a balance left. The front desk has a note in a book, but the voucher was sold at a different branch. Nobody can confirm. To avoid a scene, you honour it, and wonder how many others you have honoured twice.</p>
<h2>Why it happens</h2>
<ul><li>Balances live in notebooks or memory.</li><li>Each branch only sees its own sales.</li><li>Expiry dates are never checked, or never agreed.</li></ul>
<h2>How it is solved</h2>
<p><a href="/features/vouchers/">Vouchers</a> carry unique codes, balances, partial claims and expiry. <a href="/features/packages/">Packages</a> let you issue session bundles, redeem a session at checkout and see what remains. <a href="/features/memberships/">Memberships</a> track prepaid tiers, wallet balances, validity dates and renewals. All of it is shared across branches.</p>
<h2>A quick fix you can start today</h2>
<ol><li>Give every voucher a number and write down every use.</li><li>Put an expiry date on each one.</li><li>Keep the list somewhere every branch can read.</li></ol>
